gvproxy serves a forwarder API to the guest on the gateway IP, port 80,
with no switch to turn it off. The `expose` endpoint takes any `local`
address from the guest. A guest can open host ports on all interfaces,
create or remove unix sockets at any path the user owns, and publish
host loopback services. gvproxy also maps a virtual IP to the host
loopback by default, so the guest reaches every local service of the
host.

This adds two switches, on the command line and in the config file:
`disable-guest-api` skips the gateway listener, and `disable-host-nat`
skips the loopback mapping. packages/qemu-vm sets both. Drop this patch
when upstream ships the switches.

